  "knowledgePattern": "Reduce a large exponent using the repeating units-digit cycles",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Keep only the units digits",
      "body": "<p>Write the units digits of the first few positive powers of 2. Then do the same for powers of 3. Continue until each list repeats.</p>",
      "recap": "Multiplying the previous units digit is enough to find the next units digit.",
      "pitfall": "Start your list at power 1, not power 0.",
      "pause": "Identify and check the repeating block for each base.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Write the initial terms and check the rule to decide whether a sequence repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the position of the large exponent",
      "body": "<p>Divide 2026 by each block length. The remainder tells you where the power lies in the repeating block.</p>",
      "recap": "A distant term can be found by counting whole repeating blocks and the leftover terms.",
      "pitfall": "A zero remainder means the last position of a block.",
      "pause": "Find both units digits, add them and keep the units digit of the sum.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l17",
          "reminder": "A distant term can be found by counting whole repeating blocks and the leftover terms."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The units digits of successive positive powers of 2 are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> and then repeat, because each next digit is obtained by multiplying the previous digit by 2 and retaining its remainder modulo 10.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The units digits of powers of 3 are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and similarly repeat. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2026</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>506</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, both exponents land at the second position of their four-term cycles.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The two units digits are therefore 4 and 9. Their sum is 13, with units digit 3. Answer B.</p>"
